, 01-2024-CF-001234-A: State v. Doe – 2-Hour Hearing Requested). Ex parte Communications: All communications with the judicial office must comply with Canon 3 of the Code of Judicial Conduct, which prohibits a judge from initiating, permitting, or considering ex parte communications and from considering other communications outside the presence of the parties concerning a pending or impending proceeding, unless authorized by law. All parties must be copied on any e-mail directed to the judicial office, unless an ex parte communication is authorized by law Unsolicited Communications: Unsolicited communications from non-parties will not be considered by the Court. Parties may only contact the judicial office in accordance with these practices and procedures. E-Filing Portal Contact Information: All attorneys and self-represented litigants must provide an e-mail address to receive signed orders electronically, unless excused. Fla. R. Gen. Prac. & Jud. Admin. 2.516. It is the responsibility of attorneys and self-represented litigants to update their contact information using Form 2.603 any time there is a change in the e-mail account registered for electronic service. Response to Inquiries: If you have not received a response to an inquiry within 48 hours, you may send a follow-up email. Scheduling Procedures Court Schedule: Master Calendar – Alachua Circuit Criminal Master Calendar – Baker Circuit Court Scheduling Hearings: The judge’s scheduling calendars are not available online.  Please email FeagleR@circuit8.org to request hearing time. The email request should include: Case number, including county code Style of the case A courtesy copy of the motion(s) or petitions(s) being set The total amount of time requested from both sides Our office does not hold suggested dates for hearings and a hearing is not scheduled on the Court’s calendar unless you receive confirmation from the judicial assistant. Once a motion or petition is scheduled, any other motions or matters may NOT be noticed at the same time reserved for the first motion unless prior approval has been obtained from the judicial assistant. Please note that emergency hearings may be set by the Court without being coordinated with counsel and/or parties. Notice of Hearing: A courtesy copy of the notice of hearing should be emailed to the judicial assistant. Submission Deadlines: The court must receive all materials for the hearing no later than three business days before the hearing. Continuance Procedure: Motions to Continue received less than 48 hours prior to the court event MAY not be ruled upon prior to the court event. Parties are expected to appear if an Order to Continue is not entered. Cancelling Hearings: If a hearing is no longer needed, please notify the Court by emailing the judicial assistant a courtesy copy of the Notice of Cancellation of Hearing. Remote Appearance Remote Appearance Procedure: The Court will consider requests to appear by Zoom upon the filing of a Motion. Proposed Orders should be submitted via the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal. Requirements: If the Court grants the Motion, please email a Driver’s License or photo ID of the Zoom participant to FeagleR@circuit8.org immediately upon receipt of the proposed Order. This is required for identification purposes, or the witness can be in the presence of a Notary Public. Technology Needs: Throughout the Eighth Circuit there are two types of AV equipment available for courtroom presentation, depending on the courthouse and courtroom of your event ( please see: https://circuit8.org/departments-services/courtroom-av-equipment/ for additional information) Submission of Orders and Judgments Format: Microsoft Word Submission Method: All proposed orders must be submitted via the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal ( https://www.myflcourtaccess.com/ ). Do not send proposed orders by United States Mail or e-mail. All proposed orders must include the following coding after the body of the proposed order.  This coding allows our computer system to input the proper dates and signatures for processing. DONE AND ORDERED in Chambers at Gainesville OR Macclenny, Alachua OR Baker County, Florida on DDDD. JJJJ CERTIFICATE OF SERVICE I HEREBY CERTIFY that copies have been furnished by U.S. Mail or via filing with the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal on MMMM. CCCC AAAA For time sensitive matters that require immediate attention, please email FeagleR@circuit8.org to advise that a proposed Order has been uploaded to the portal. Deadline for Submissions: Proposed orders must be submitted within 10 days after any hearing. Courtesy Copies of Case Law and Other Documents When Required: Courtesy copies of case law or other legal authority must be submitted to the Court for any evidentiary proceeding. Format: PDF format Submission Method: E-Mail Deadline for Submission: Courtesy copies must be e-mailed to FeagleR@circuit8.org no later than three (3) business days before any evidentiary proceeding. Please also bring a hard copy to the hearing. Emergency and Other Urgent Matters Requirements: If a party believes there is a factual basis for setting an emergency hearing, a detailed motion setting forth the following must be filed: (1) the issues to be resolved, (2) reasons why an emergency hearing is necessary, and (3) the amount of time needed for each party’s presentation. Scheduling: If the court determines that an emergency exists, a hearing will be scheduled unilaterally by the court. All parties shall make themselves available for the emergency hearing, barring exigent circumstances. Pretrial Procedures and Conferences Case Management Conference: In Alachua County, in-custody Defendants will be transported for Case Management Dockets. The Sheriff’s Office is authorized to not transport inmates if their attorney waives their appearance during the morning docket and communicates same to the Sheriff’s Office. In Baker County, in-custody Defendants will appear remotely from the jail. A Motion and proposed Order to Transport is required if their attorney requests an in-person appearance. Pre-Trial Conference: The Court requires all Defendants and Attorneys of Record to appear in-person for Pre-Trial Conferences. Their appearance may not be waived. The Court encourages parties to file and schedule all substantive pre-trial motions prior to the Pre-Trial Conference. Changes of Plea: Changes of plea may be heard at both the Criminal General and Case Management Conference dockets. Any plea expected to last more than 15 minutes should be specially set on the judge’s calendar. Parties should email FeagleR@circuit8.org to specially set. Parties should have a completed plea petition with the applicable attachments at the time of the scheduled court event. Substitution of Counsel: Stipulations should be signed by the defendant, current and new counsel. New counsel shall file the signed stipulation and Motion and submit a proposed Order via the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal. The Court will require a hearing if all signatures are not present. Coverage Attorneys: Pursuant to Administrative Order No. 1.30, “coverage” attorneys must file a notice of appearance as co-counsel before the Court will recognize the attorney as representing that party. A l ink to Administrative Order No. 1.30 is included below within the Forms and Administrative Orders tab . Forms and Administrative Orders Access: Find Circuit Criminal forms on our Forms and Checklists page . Court Reporting Administrative Order No. 1.30 (Notice to Appear/Withdraw Requirements for All Cases) Other Division Procedures ADA Accommodations: Under the Americans with Disabilities Act , if you are a person with a disability who needs any accommodation in order to participate in a proceeding, you are entitled to be provided with certain assistance at no cost to you. Please contact the ADA Coordinator, Alachua County Family and Civil Justice Center, 201 East University Avenue, Gainesville, FL 32601 ( 352-337-6237 ) at least 7 days before your scheduled court appearance, or immediately upon receiving this notification if the time before the scheduled appearance is less than 7 days. If you are hearing or voice-impaired, call 1-800-955-8770 via Florida Relay Service. Interpreter Requests: All requests should be made in advance whenever possible and in writing to Court Administration via e-mail at: interpreter@circuit8.org and copied to Raina Feagle at: FeagleR@circuit8.org .  If you do not receive a confirmation request within two business days prior to the hearing or event, please contact the Court Interpreter at (352) 264-7063. If the Court Interpreter is unavailable, contact Court Administration at (352) 264-7029. Please be sure to include the following information when making your request: Date, time and location of the event Language desired, include any geographical preferences (ex. Brazilian Portuguese) The case number and name of the person(s) requiring interpretation The name and telephone of the person making the request The type of event and any special needs or instructions Courts & Judges Court Calendars Jurors Departments & Services Self-Help Contact & Information Accessibility Policy Skip Navigation Courts & Judges Eighth Judicial Circuit About Administrative Judges Employment Circuit Judges Intranet (Restricted) Senior Judges Judicial Assignments General Magistrates and Hearing Officers Alachua County Baker County Bradford County Gilchrist County Levy County Union County About About About About About About Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Calendars Weekly Calendars Master Calendars Holidays Jurors Jury Info Learn more jury duty information by county. Jury Excusal View our Jury Excusal page for county-specific excusal information. FAQ's View frequently asked questions about jury duty. Departments & Services Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) Informatio